time limit per test: 4 seconds
memory limit per test: 256 megabytes

You are given an integer n and an array a of length n - 1.
For a permutation* p of length n, define
vi = min(max(1<=j<=i) pj, max(i+1<=j<=n) pj),
where 1 <= i <= n - 1.
In other words, cut the permutation between positions i and i + 1, take the maximum element on each side, and let vi be the smaller of these two values.
Your task is to count the number of permutations p of length n such that vi = ai for each 1 <= i <= n - 1.
Output the answer modulo 998 244 353.
    A permutation of length n is an array consisting of n distinct integers from 1 to n in arbitrary order. For example, [2, 3, 1, 5, 4] is a permutation, but [1, 2, 2] is not a permutation (2 appears twice in the array), and [1, 3, 4] is also not a permutation (n = 3 but there is 4 in the array).

Input:
Each test contains multiple test cases. The first line contains the number of test cases t (1 <= t <= 10^4). The description of the test cases follows.
The first line of each test case contains one integer n (2 <= n <= 10^6) — the length of p.
The second line contains n - 1 integers a1, a2, ..., an-1 (1 <= ai <= n) — the elements of a.
It is guaranteed that the sum of n over all test cases does not exceed 10^6.

Output:
For each test case, output one integer — the number of suitable permutations, modulo 998 244 353.

Strategy:
This is a combinatorics problem. Is idea is that we either have spots where there can only be one number and then spots where can be any number. In each spot where the maximum changes (meaning vi is different than v(i+1)), we know the number at that spot i+1 is v(i+1). This also means the shape of the values in the array will be a sort of a mountain with a peak. This is because first we ascend because we are increasing the maximum by adding new bigger numbers until we reach the peak n. At this peak the left side will have n and therefore the min condition will take the right side as the result, since we are removing numbers from the right side by shifting even more to the right, the value will never increase. We can leverage this. First we check this, if any of this is false for the input we print zero. Namely, we check that the array in non-decreasing, then reaches a peak and then is strictly non-increasing. Then we run a loop to count the numbers, if they dont include n-1 (which has to be there for when n is on one side and n-1 on the other) or if they include n (which is impossible since we will always have a min of n-1 at most, we output 0. We have a base total of 2 since the n is either on the left and n-1 on the right or the opposite. Then, we just loop through number n-1 to 1. Inside the loop we check if there are any numbers of this value in v, if there are we add cnt[num]-1 to our counter of numbers that don't have any set position (unlike the last one which needs to be at the step where vi is num). If there are no numbers of the value we need to pick one. For this we have __available count of options. We multiply total by that with mod and decrease __available by 1. We do this until the end of the loop. Print total.